    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0360" n="242" sameAs="frus:frus1929v03/pg242"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.9412Anti-War/98: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neville</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-03-19T11:00:00+09:00">March 19, 1929—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 19—2:11 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>25. Department’s March 18, 1 p.m. The <hi rendition="#italic">News</hi> as quoted
                    is incorrect. The Prime Minister or some parliamentary representative of the
                    Government is constantly being questioned in the Diet. On the 14th the Prime
                    Minister in reply to a question stated briefly that the Government is taking the
                    necessary procedure to ratify the treaty and believes that it contains nothing
                    contrary to the Constitution. This is the stock answer to all such questions.
                    The <hi rendition="#italic">Daily News</hi> man evidently <pb facs="0361"
                        n="243" xml:id="pg243"/> got hold of a wrong translation that day and
                    thought it was something new.</p>
                <p>As I have previously explained, the Government desires to avoid discussing the
                    treaty in the Diet.</p>
                <p>I shall telegraph further in regard to the instruction.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Neville</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
